scaletta:
CRM example with the request "prepare the follow-ups for this week's hot leads":
- the chatbot writes a generic email;
- the agent queries the CRM, filters the leads, drafts personalised emails and asks for approval.

P1.4.animazione:
- What you see:
- The diagram of an agent breaks into 4 blocks that move apart, each with a label and an icon: chip = model, spanner = tools, stylised orchestra conductor = orchestration, filing cabinet = memory.
- Interaction:
- The student can switch off one block at a time and launch the same task ("qualify the new leads"):
- without tools, the agent produces only hypothetical text;
- without orchestration, it stops after one step or ignores the limits;
- without memory, it contacts the same lead twice;
- without the model, nothing decides.
- Each fault is shown with an icon and a sentence.
- Why it helps: "what happens if it is missing" fixes the function of each component better than a definition.

P1.5.animazione:
- What you see:
- A horizontal slider with 5 marks. Above it there is a process diagram that changes:
- mark 1: a flowchart with fixed arrows;
- mark 2: an "AI" box in a single node;
- mark 3: AI boxes in several nodes, but arrows still fixed;
- mark 4: the arrows become dashed and are "drawn" at runtime by the agent;
- mark 5: several agents exchange messages.
- On the side, four indicators move together with the slider: flexibility ↑, predictability ↓, cost ↑, risk ↑.
- Interaction: the student drags three scenarios (e.g. "sending the same monthly newsletter to everyone", "replying to very variable quote requests", "summarising a call") onto the mark they think is right and receives reasoned feedback.
- Why it helps: it shows the trade-off visually. Autonomy is not "better" in absolute terms.
